    intro: 'The superior view on the left carries every part the labelling exercise asks for. Walk the ring from the front: body, then the pedicle bridging back from it, the lamina closing the arch, and the spinous process pointing out behind — with the vertebral foramen enclosed in the middle for the spinal cord.',
    key: [
      { mark: 'Body', name: 'Anterior; the primary weight-bearing part (label B… in the exercise, described by position)' },
      { mark: 'Vertebral foramen', name: 'The opening the spinal cord passes through (B1)' },
      { mark: 'Pedicle', name: 'Short bridge running back from the body (B2); part of the vertebral arch' },
      { mark: 'Lamina', name: 'Flat plate closing the arch behind (B5); part of the vertebral arch' },
      { mark: 'Transverse process', name: 'Lateral projection, one each side (B3)' },
      { mark: 'Spinous process', name: 'Single backward projection you can feel through the skin (B4)' },
      { mark: 'Facet of superior articular process', name: 'Forms the facet joint with the vertebra above (B6)' },
      { mark: 'Inferior articular process', name: 'Forms the facet joint with the vertebra below' },
      { mark: 'Intervertebral disc', name: 'The shock absorber between two vertebral bodies (side view)' },
      { mark: 'Spinal nerve / intervertebral foramen', name: 'A spinal nerve leaves between two vertebrae — a different opening from the vertebral foramen' },
      { mark: 'Facet for head of rib', name: 'Present on thoracic vertebrae only', beyond: true },
    ],
  },
  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
   * The 2026 cell-biology and tissue set.
   *
   * These lessons shipped with no picture at all, on the reasoning that
   * nothing on disk depicted a membrane or a Golgi apparatus. That was true
   * of what was on disk and false of what was available: the whole OpenStax
   * Anatomy & Physiology figure set is on Commons under CC BY, and OpenStax
   * A&P 2e is the free textbook the HSS2011 orientation deck itself names.
   * So these are the recommended textbook's own figures, and every key below
   * was read off the image rather than from memory of what it probably shows.
   * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  levelsOfOrganisation: {
    file: 'levels-of-organisation.jpg', bytes: 496271,
    title: 'The levels of organisation',
    caption: 'Atoms to organism as a pyramid, each level built out of the one above it, worked through a single example — smooth muscle, bladder, urinary system.',
    author: 'OpenStax',
    licence: 'CC BY 4.0',
    licenceUrl: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0',
    commons: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:101_Levels_of_Org_in_Body.jpg',
    intro: 'The lesson’s chain as one figure, and what makes it worth a picture is that the SAME tissue is followed the whole way down. Smooth muscle cell → smooth muscle tissue → the bladder wall → the urinary system → the person drinking. Read top to bottom and each band is the one above it repeated and assembled.',
    key: [
      { mark: 'Cellular level', name: 'Molecules combine into the fluid and organelles of a body cell — here a smooth muscle cell, with organelles and cell fluid marked' },
      { mark: 'Tissue level', name: 'A community of SIMILAR cells: smooth muscle tissue. This is the level the four tissue types classify' },
      { mark: 'Organ level', name: 'Two or more DIFFERENT tissues combine — the bladder, carrying both smooth muscle and skeletal muscle' },
      { mark: 'Organ system level', name: 'Two or more organs working together: the urinary tract — kidney, ureter, bladder, urethra' },
      { mark: 'Organismal level', name: 'Many organ systems working together as an independent organism' },
      { mark: 'Chemical level', name: 'Atoms bond into molecules — hydrogen and oxygen making water. Below where this lecture starts', beyond: true },
    ],
  },
  plasmaMembrane: {
    file: 'plasma-membrane.jpg', bytes: 397954,
    title: 'What is actually in the plasma membrane',
    caption: 'The bilayer cut open, with every component the lecture lists drawn in place: two kinds of protein, two carbohydrate-carrying molecules, and cholesterol between the tails.',
    author: 'OpenStax',
    licence: 'CC BY 4.0',
    licenceUrl: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0',
    commons: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0303_Lipid_Bilayer_With_Various_Components.jpg',
    intro: 'Read it as the lecture’s slide 9 list made visible. The two rows of red heads with tails facing each other are the bilayer — 2 nm thick. Everything else is stuck into or onto it, and the distinction that matters is INTEGRAL (spans the whole thickness) against PERIPHERAL (sits on one face only). Notice that every branched chain points the same way: outward. That is the glycocalyx, and it is why a cell can be recognised from outside.',
    key: [
      { mark: 'Phospholipid bilayer', name: 'Two sheets of phospholipid, heads out and tails inward. The lecture measures it at 2 nm' },
      { mark: 'Integral membrane protein', name: 'Spans the full thickness. The carrier, channel, enzyme and receptor classes are all of this kind' },
      { mark: 'Peripheral membrane protein', name: 'Attached to ONE face only, not crossing — drawn here on the inner surface' },
      { mark: 'Channel protein', name: 'An integral protein with a pore through it. A gated channel is one that can shut' },
      { mark: 'Glycoprotein', name: 'Protein with carbohydrate attached — half the glycocalyx, and how a recognition protein identifies the cell' },
      { mark: 'Glycolipid', name: 'Lipid with carbohydrate attached — the other half of the glycocalyx' },
      { mark: 'Cholesterol', name: 'The small pale molecules wedged between the tails, keeping the membrane fluid rather than rigid' },
    ],
  },
  animalCell: {
    file: 'animal-cell.jpg', bytes: 397587,
    title: 'A model cell and its organelles',
    caption: 'The prototypical human cell with every organelle labelled — including the ones the app was previously folding away as prior knowledge.',
    author: 'OpenStax',
    licence: 'CC BY 4.0',
    licenceUrl: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0',
    commons: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0312_Animal_Cell_and_Components.jpg',
    intro: 'Sort what you see by the lecture’s own organising question: does it have a membrane around it? Nucleus, ER, Golgi, mitochondria, lysosomes and peroxisomes do — those are compartments. Ribosomes, the cytoskeleton and the centrosome do not — those are structures. Then find the three cytoskeletal elements separately: they are drawn in three different colours and thicknesses, and the single word "cytoskeleton" hides that it is three things.',
    key: [
      { mark: 'Nucleus', name: 'The largest organelle, membrane-bound; holds the chromatin and the nucleolus' },
      { mark: 'Nucleolus', name: 'The dense body inside the nucleus where ribosomes are assembled' },
      { mark: 'Chromatin', name: 'DNA with its proteins, uncondensed — what a chromosome looks like when the cell is not dividing' },
      { mark: 'Rough endoplasmic reticulum', name: 'Studded with ribosomes (the red dots). Modifies and packages newly synthesised PROTEIN' },
      { mark: 'Smooth endoplasmic reticulum', name: 'No ribosomes. Synthesises LIPID. Same organelle, split by cargo rather than by looks' },
      { mark: 'Ribosomes', name: 'Non-membranous. Free in the cytoplasm as well as bound to the rough ER' },
      { mark: 'Golgi apparatus', name: 'The stacks of flattened membranes — cisternae. Stores, alters and packages secretions AND lysosomal enzymes' },
      { mark: 'Lysosome', name: 'Membrane-bound bag of digestive enzymes' },
      { mark: 'Peroxisome', name: 'Named separately from the lysosome: catabolism of fats, and neutralising the toxins that produces' },
      { mark: 'Mitochondria', name: 'Double-membraned; the folded inner membrane is drawn' },
      { mark: 'Centrosome', name: 'The centriole pair — non-membranous. Organises the spindle in mitosis, and the microtubules the rest of the time' },
      { mark: 'Microtubule', name: 'The thickest cytoskeletal element — the "slender tubes" of the lecture’s definition' },
      { mark: 'Microfilament', name: 'The thinnest — the "fine filaments"' },
      { mark: 'Intermediate filament', name: 'Between the two in diameter; the one that mostly bears strain' },
      { mark: 'Plasma membrane', name: 'The boundary. The membrane lesson’s figure cuts this open' },
      { mark: 'Cytoplasm', name: 'Everything inside the membrane except the nucleus — cytosol plus the organelles in it' },
      { mark: 'Secretory vesicle / Golgi vesicle', name: 'Pinched off the Golgi, carrying its cargo out to the membrane', beyond: true },
      { mark: 'Vacuole', name: 'A storage sac — prominent in plant cells, small and occasional in human ones', beyond: true },
    ],
  },
  cellNucleus: {
    file: 'cell-nucleus.jpg', bytes: 363052,
    title: 'Inside the nucleus',
    caption: 'The nucleus cut open — double envelope, pores through it, chromatin filling it, nucleolus inside — running straight on into the rough ER around it.',
    author: 'OpenStax',
    licence: 'CC BY 4.0',
    licenceUrl: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0',
    commons: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0318_Nucleus.jpg',
    intro: 'Two things here are easy to read off the picture and hard to get from words. First, the envelope is DOUBLE, and at the bottom right it continues without a break into the rough ER — the nucleus is not a separate bag, it is part of the same membrane system. Second, the pores are holes, not doors: mRNA leaves through them, which is the whole reason transcription can happen in here and translation out there.',
    key: [
      { mark: 'Nuclear envelope', name: 'Two membranes, not one, enclosing the nucleus' },
      { mark: 'Nuclear pores', name: 'Openings through both membranes — the exit mRNA uses, and the only way through for anything large' },
      { mark: 'Chromatin (condensed)', name: 'The DNA-plus-protein filling the nucleus. It packs tighter still to become a visible chromosome' },
      { mark: 'Nucleolus', name: 'The dense sphere inside — no membrane of its own; where ribosomal subunits are built' },
      { mark: 'Cisternae', name: 'The flattened sacs of the rough ER, continuous with the outer nuclear membrane', beyond: true },
    ],
  },
  mrnaSplicing: {
    file: 'mrna-splicing.jpg', bytes: 136732,
    title: 'Introns removed, exons spliced together',
    caption: 'The step the lecture states in six words, drawn: a transcript arrives in pieces, the introns are cut out, and the exons are joined into one continuous message.',
    author: 'OpenStax',
    licence: 'CC BY 4.0',
    licenceUrl: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0',
    commons: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0326_Splicing.jpg',
    intro: 'Compare the top bar with the bottom one and the whole idea is there. The top is the pre-mRNA as first copied off the gene: exon, intron, exon, intron, exon — five blocks, alternating. The bottom is what actually leaves the nucleus: Exon 1, 2 and 3 pushed together with nothing between them. The middle shows where the difference is made, and the loop at bottom left is the intron itself, cut out and thrown away. EXons EXit; INtrons stay IN.',
    key: [
      { mark: 'pre-mRNA transcript', name: 'The first copy, straight off the gene — still carrying both kinds of block' },
      { mark: 'Exon 1 / Exon 2 / Exon 3', name: 'The kept pieces. Joined end to end, they carry the protein’s code' },
      { mark: 'Intron', name: 'The discarded pieces. Between the exons in the transcript, absent from the product' },
      { mark: 'Spliced RNA', name: 'The finished mRNA: three exons, no introns, one continuous instruction' },
      { mark: 'Spliceosome', name: 'The machine doing the cutting and rejoining — named on the figure, not in the lecture', beyond: true },
    ],
  },
  mitosisStages: {
    file: 'mitosis-stages.jpg', bytes: 707901,
    title: 'The stages of mitosis and cytokinesis',
    caption: 'Six columns — five of mitosis, then cytokinesis — each with a drawing, what happens, and a fluorescence micrograph of a real cell doing it.',
    author: 'OpenStax',
    licence: 'CC BY 4.0',
    licenceUrl: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0',
    commons: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0331_Stages_of_Mitosis_and_Cytokinesis.jpg',
    intro: 'Read the bottom row first. Those are photographs of real dividing cells at 5 µm scale, and they are what the drawings above them are drawings OF — the green is the spindle, the blue is the DNA. Then read the bracket along the very bottom: it stops before the last column. Cytokinesis is not part of mitosis. Mitosis divides the nucleus, cytokinesis divides the cell, and they are counted separately for a reason.',
    key: [
      { mark: 'Prophase', name: 'Chromosomes condense and become visible; spindle fibres emerge; the nuclear envelope breaks down' },
      { mark: 'Metaphase', name: 'Chromosomes line up on the metaphase plate, each sister chromatid attached to a fibre from the opposite pole' },
      { mark: 'Anaphase', name: 'Centromeres split; the sister chromatids — now chromosomes in their own right — are pulled to opposite poles' },
      { mark: 'Telophase', name: 'Chromosomes arrive and decondense; nuclear envelope re-forms; the spindle breaks down' },
      { mark: 'Cytokinesis', name: 'OUTSIDE the mitosis bracket. A cleavage furrow pinches an animal cell into two' },
      { mark: 'Prometaphase', name: 'Between prophase and metaphase — kinetochores appear and the spindle attaches. Usually folded into prophase in a four-stage telling', beyond: true },
    ],
  },
  epitheliumTypes: {
    file: 'epithelium-types.jpg', bytes: 577301,
    title: 'Every epithelium, by shape and by layers',
    caption: 'The whole classification as a table — each type drawn, with where it is found and what it does.',
    author: 'OpenStax College',
    licence: 'CC BY 3.0',
    licenceUrl: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0',
    commons: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:423_Table_04_02_Summary_of_Epithelial_Tissue_CellsN.jpg',
    intro: 'The lecture classifies epithelium twice over — by cell SHAPE (squamous flat, cuboidal square, columnar tall) and by LAYERS (simple is one, stratified is many) — and every name is just those two answers joined. Use the drawings to fix it: compare the first three rows, all simple, and only the shape changes. Then compare row 1 with row 5, both squamous, and only the number of layers changes. The location column is the part worth memorising, because location is what a question hands you.',
    key: [
      { mark: 'Simple squamous', name: 'One layer of flat cells — air sacs of the lungs, lining of heart and vessels. Thin enough to diffuse across' },
      { mark: 'Simple cuboidal', name: 'One layer of cube-shaped cells — gland ducts and kidney tubules. Secretes and absorbs' },
      { mark: 'Simple columnar', name: 'One layer of tall cells — digestive tract, bronchi, uterine tubes. Absorbs; secretes mucus and enzymes' },
      { mark: 'Stratified squamous', name: 'Many layers, flat at the surface — oesophagus, mouth. Protects against abrasion' },
      { mark: 'Stratified cuboidal', name: 'Many layers of cube-shaped cells — sweat, salivary and mammary glands' },
      { mark: 'Stratified columnar', name: 'Many layers, tall at the surface — male urethra and some gland ducts' },
      { mark: 'Pseudostratified columnar', name: 'ONE layer that looks like several, because the nuclei sit at different heights — trachea and upper airway', beyond: true },
      { mark: 'Transitional', name: 'Changes shape as the organ stretches — bladder, ureters, urethra', beyond: true },
    ],
  },
  negativeFeedback: {
    file: 'negative-feedback.jpg', bytes: 168432,
    title: 'A negative feedback loop, twice',
    caption: 'The general loop on the left, and on the right the same loop with body-temperature control filled into it.',
    author: 'OpenStax',
    licence: 'CC BY 4.0',
    licenceUrl: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0',
    commons: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:105_Negative_Feedback_Loops.jpg',
    intro: 'Two diagrams of one thing, side by side — that is the point of the figure, so read them ACROSS rather than down. Sensor is the nerve cells; control is the centre in the brain; effector is the sweat glands. The thick red arrow returning to the top is what makes the loop negative: the effector’s action removes the stimulus that started it, so the loop switches itself off.',
    key: [
      { mark: 'Stimulus', name: 'The change away from the set point — here, body temperature exceeding 37 °C' },
      { mark: 'Sensor', name: 'Detects the change — nerve cells in the skin and brain' },
      { mark: 'Control', name: 'Compares against the set point and decides — the temperature regulatory centre in the brain' },
      { mark: 'Effector', name: 'Acts to reverse the change — sweat glands throughout the body' },
      { mark: 'The return arrow', name: 'The effector’s action cancels the stimulus. NEGATIVE means opposing, not harmful' },
    ],
  },
  boneCells: {
    file: 'bone-cells.jpg', bytes: 202395,
    title: 'The four bone cells',
    caption: 'All four drawn where they actually live in the bone, each with its one-line job.',
    author: 'OpenStax College',
    licence: 'CC BY 3.0',
    licenceUrl: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0',
    commons: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:604_Bone_cells.jpg',
    intro: 'The arrows matter as much as the cells: they come off three different places in the cut bone, so you can see these four are not interchangeable — they sit in different positions. Then read the shapes, because shape tells you the job. The osteocyte is the spidery one, walled into the matrix with processes reaching out through it; the osteoclast is the big one with a ruffled edge pressed against the bone it is dissolving.',
    key: [
      { mark: 'Osteogenic cell', name: 'The stem cell. The only one of the four that divides; it becomes an osteoblast' },
      { mark: 'Osteoblast', name: 'Forms bone matrix. Blast BUILDS' },
      { mark: 'Osteocyte', name: 'A former osteoblast, now walled into the matrix it laid down; maintains bone tissue' },
      { mark: 'Osteoclast', name: 'Resorbs bone — dissolves matrix and releases the minerals. Clast CRACKS. Note the ruffled border against the bone' },
    ],
  },
  boneMarrow: {
    file: 'bone-marrow.jpg', bytes: 219580,
    title: 'Red and yellow marrow, in a real bone',
    caption: 'The cut head of a long bone, photographed. The two marrows are different colours and the boundary between them is visible.',
    author: 'OpenStax College',
    licence: 'CC BY 3.0',
    licenceUrl: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0',
    commons: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:619_Red_and_Yellow_Bone_Marrow.jpg',
    intro: 'A photograph rather than a diagram, because the distinction really is a visible colour. Red marrow is blood-forming tissue and fills the spongy bone toward the outside; yellow marrow is fat, and sits centrally. With age the red retreats and the yellow spreads, which is why "where is red marrow found" has a different answer in an adult than in a child.',
    key: [
      { mark: 'Red marrow', name: 'Haematopoietic — makes blood cells. Occupies the spaces in spongy bone' },
      { mark: 'Yellow marrow', name: 'Mostly adipose. Fills the medullary cavity, and replaces red marrow with age' },
      { mark: 'Outer surface of bone', name: 'The compact bone shell, with periosteum over it' },
    ],
  },
  cartilageTypes: {
    file: 'cartilage-types.jpg', bytes: 400240,
    title: 'The three cartilages',
    caption: 'Hyaline, fibrocartilage and elastic — each as a drawing beside the stained section it was drawn from.',
    author: 'OpenStax College',
    licence: 'CC BY 3.0',
    licenceUrl: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0',
    commons: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:412_Types_of_Cartilage-new.jpg',
    intro: 'Three rows, and in each one the drawing on the left is a reading of the photograph on the right. What separates the three is not the cells — every row has the same chondrocytes sitting in the same small cavities — but what lies BETWEEN them. Hyaline: a smooth, near-featureless matrix. Fibrocartilage: thick collagen in visible parallel bundles, which is why it takes compression in the knee and the discs. Elastic: a dense tangle of elastic fibres, which is why an ear springs back.',
    key: [
      { mark: 'Hyaline cartilage', name: 'Glassy, uniform matrix. The commonest — joint surfaces, costal cartilage, the airway rings' },
      { mark: 'Fibrocartilage', name: 'Heavy parallel collagen bundles. Where compression has to be absorbed — intervertebral discs, knee menisci' },
      { mark: 'Elastic cartilage', name: 'A network of elastic fibres. Where the shape has to spring back — the external ear, the epiglottis' },
      { mark: 'Chondrocytes in lacunae', name: 'The cartilage cell, each in its own small cavity in the matrix. Common to all three rows', beyond: true },
    ],
  },
};

export function figureFor(id) {
  const f = FIGURES[id];
  return f ? { ...f, src: 'assets/figures/' + f.file } : null;
}

/* True where the app still draws it itself, because there is nothing to depict. */
export function isHandDrawn(id) { return !FIGURES[id]; }
